1. A CURRENCY KEY IS A ROW IN TCURC; ITS DECIMALS ARE A ROW IN TCURX
   key    ISO   decimals   source of the decimals
   EUR    EUR      2       default (not in TCURX)
   USD    USD      2       default (not in TCURX)
   JPY    JPY      0       TCURX
   KWD    KWD      3       TCURX
   CLP    CLP      0       TCURX
   USDN   USD      5       TCURX
   Two decimals is not stored anywhere: it is what a missing TCURX row means.

2. THE DATABASE HOLDS EVERY AMOUNT WITH TWO DECIMALS, SHIFTED
   what the user entered        what BSEG-WRBTR holds      shift
                1,000 JPY   ->          10.00        x 10^-2
                1.500 KWD   ->          15.00        x 10^1
             1,234.56 EUR   ->        1234.56        none
              250,000 CLP   ->        2500.00        x 10^-2
             0.12345 USDN   ->         123.45        x 10^3
   1,000 JPY and 10.00 EUR are the same bytes in the amount column. Only the
   currency key beside it tells them apart.

3. THE SHIFT IS REVERSIBLE, AND EVERY OUTPUT HAS TO REVERSE IT
        10.00 + JPY   ->          1,000 JPY   round trip ok
        15.00 + KWD   ->          1.500 KWD   round trip ok
      1234.56 + EUR   ->       1,234.56 EUR   round trip ok
      2500.00 + CLP   ->        250,000 CLP   round trip ok
       123.45 + USDN  ->       0.12345 USDN   round trip ok

4. THE CLASSIC BUG: READ THE COLUMN, FORGET THE KEY
   SE16 on BSEG shows WRBTR = 10.00 for a 1,000 JPY item, and that is correct.
   A report that prints the field as a plain number says  10.00
   A report that applies the key says                    1,000 JPY
   A report that 'fixes' it by multiplying every JPY row by 100 and then
   also prints WITH the key says                          100,000 JPY
   All three are plausible numbers. Only one of them is the invoice.

5. THE SAME SHIFT, SEEN ON THE SOURCE DOCUMENT'S SCREENSHOT
   Company code Z013 has local currency JPY. A 100 USD document at 1 USD = 100 JPY
   is 100.00 USD = 10,000 JPY.  In the raw BSEG display it appears as:
     WRBTR (document currency, USD) = 100.00
     DMBTR (local currency, JPY)    = 100.00
   Both columns read 100,00 on the screen, and the JPY one means ten thousand.

6. YOU CANNOT ADD A COLUMN OF STORED AMOUNTS ACROSS CURRENCIES
   rows:  1,000 JPY, 10.00 EUR, 1.500 KWD
   sum of the stored values = 35.00   (three currencies, one meaningless number)
   Every SAP total is per currency key, or converted into one currency first.

7. TWO BAPIs THAT EXIST BECAUSE OF THIS TABLE
   BAPI_CURRENCY_CONV_TO_INTERNAL  external -> stored   (what a screen field feeds to the DB)
   BAPI_CURRENCY_CONV_TO_EXTERNAL  stored -> external   (what an interface must call before sending)
   BAPI_CURRENCY_GETDECIMALS       the TCURX lookup on its own
   e.g. CONV_TO_EXTERNAL(15.00, KWD) = 1.500 KWD;  CONV_TO_INTERNAL(1,000 JPY) = 10.00
